===Usage notes===
In respect of general adjectival and adverbial use, some usage guides distinguish {{m|en|farther}} and {{m|en||further}}, with ''farther'' referring to distance, and ''further'' referring to degree or time.<ref name="grammargirl">{{cite-web |titleurl=https://www.quickanddirtytips.com/articles/further-versus-farther |work=Quick and Dirty Tips |title=Grammar Girl: Further Versus Farther |accessdate=4 June 2009 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090520141152/http://grammar.quickanddirtytips.com/further-versus-farther.aspx |archivedate=20 May 2009 }}</ref> Others, such as the OED, recommend ''farther'' as a comparative form of ''far'' and ''further'' for use when it is not comparative.<ref>[http://www.dailywritingtips.com/farther-further-whats-the-difference/ Daily Writing Tips – Farther, Further: What’s the Difference?]</ref> However, most sources consider the two interchangeable in most or all circumstances,<ref>[[w:Fowler's Modern English Usage|Fowler’s ''Modern English Usage'']]</ref> and historically, they were not distinguished.<ref name="grammargirl" /><ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|farther}}</ref>

''Farther'' is uncommon or old-fashioned in certain subsidiary senses, such as the adjectival sense of "more, additional" and the adverbial sense "moreover." It is virtually never used as a replacement for "further" in the phrase "further to."

As a verb, ''further'' greatly predominates over ''farther'' in modern English.

